  "Question": "If \\(x^{-1}-1\\) is divided by \\(x-1\\) the quotient is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac1{x-1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(-\\dfrac1{x-1}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac1x\\)",
    "E": "\\(-\\dfrac1x\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Since \\(x^{-1}=1/x\\), the original expression requires \\(x\\ne0\\). Division by \\(x-1\\) also requires \\(x\\ne1\\).\n\nRewrite the numerator to expose a factor of \\(x-1\\):\n\\[\nx^{-1}-1=\\frac{1-x}{x}=-\\frac{x-1}{x}.\n\\]\nThe quotient is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{-1}-1}{x-1}\n=-\\frac{x-1}{x(x-1)}\n=-\\frac1x,\n\\]\nwhere cancellation is valid because \\(x-1\\ne0\\). Thus the answer is \\(-1/x\\), with \\(x\\ne0,1\\), choice E.",
